<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Parallel Thinking, Sequential Answering: Bridging NAR and AR for Efficient Reasoning</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Qihang Ai
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Nanyang Technological University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">qihang005@e.ntu.edu.sg</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Haiyun Jiang
</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding author.</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Shanghai Jiao Tong University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">haiyun2025@sjtu.edu.cn</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We study reasoning tasks through a framework that integrates auto-regressive (AR) and non-autoregressive (NAR) language models. AR models, which generate text sequentially, excel at producing coherent outputs but often suffer from slow inference—particularly in reasoning-intensive domains such as mathematics and code, where lengthy chains of thought are required. In contrast, NAR models, such as discrete diffusion models, allow parallel generation and offer substantial speedups, though typically at the cost of reduced output quality. To address these limitations, we introduce a new paradigm in which an NAR model efficiently produces intermediate reasoning traces, which subsequently guide an AR model to deliver precise final answers. Experiments demonstrate that our approach yields significant 26% improvements over strong baselines while substantially reducing inference cost.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, Large Language Models (LLMs) with an emphasis on reasoning—often termed Large Reasoning Models (LRMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2025a)</a></cite>—have achieved significant progress on complex reasoning tasks. A representative example is DeepSeek-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. (2025)</a></cite>, which employs reinforcement learning (RL) with outcome-based and verifiable rewards to explicitly encourage the development of reasoning skills.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">DeepSeek-R1 and its distilled variants generally adopt a structured generation scheme following a <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;think&gt;</span>–<span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;answer&gt;</span> format. In this framework, the <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;think&gt;</span> stage produces detailed step-by-step reasoning traces, which then guide the <span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;answer&gt;</span> stage to yield the final solution. This procedure aligns with the widely used Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. (2022)</a></cite>, and models adopting such an approach are commonly referred to as R1-style models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Building on this observation, an increasingly popular inference paradigm has been to enhance model performance by allocating more tokens to the reasoning stage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024)</a></cite>. While this strategy can improve solution quality, it also introduces practical challenges: the generation of excessively long reasoning sequences increases computational overhead and inference latency. This overproduction of reasoning tokens often leads to the so-called overthinking problem, where many intermediate steps turn out to be redundant or uninformative <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sui et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address the inefficiency stemming from prolonged reasoning, recent research has shifted toward more economical reasoning strategies, including hybrid methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025a)</a></cite> that balance explicit reasoning with concise inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Renze and Guven (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2025b)</a></cite>, thereby mitigating the high computational cost associated with extended token generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">However, in challenging reasoning tasks—such as mathematics and programming—that inherently demand multi-step reasoning, explicit thinking becomes indispensable. The Chain-of-Thought paradigm has been widely adopted to enhance the ability of LLMs in tackling these problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2025b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kojima et al. (2022)</a></cite>. In addition, some works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. (2023)</a></cite> highlight a complementary line of research on code training benefits for mathematical reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Non-autoregressive (NAR) generation offers an alternative to traditional left-to-right decoding by allowing tokens to be predicted and refined in parallel. A key line of work in this paradigm is diffusion language models (DLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepMind (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025b)</a></cite>, which cast text generation as a denoising process that iteratively transforms noise into fluent outputs. Such models bring several benefits for reasoning: parallel generation substantially reduces inference latency, iterative refinement provides a natural mechanism to correct low-confidence predictions, and the bidirectional conditioning strengthens global coherence.
Recent progress, such as Seed Diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al. (2025)</a></cite>, Mercury Coder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Labs (2025)</a></cite>, and Gemini Diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepMind (2025)</a></cite>, has further demonstrated that large-scale DLMs can achieve both high-quality outputs and high-speed inference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by these advantages, we propose a hybrid reasoning paradigm that divides labor between NAR and AR models. Specifically, the NAR component generates the <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">think</span> stage—compact but explicit reasoning traces—while the AR component produces the <span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">answer</span> stage, ensuring precise and faithful final outputs. This design inherits the complementary strengths of both approaches: the efficiency and global context modeling of NAR, together with the reliability and expressiveness of AR. In summary, our contribution is a new reasoning framework that combines NAR and AR models to achieve higher accuracy while significantly reducing inference cost.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Diffusion Language Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Autoregressive (AR) models dominate current large language models such as ChatGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achiam et al. (2023)</a></cite>, generating text in a strictly sequential token-by-token manner.
However, this token-by-token paradigm inherently limits inference speed.
Diffusion Language Models (DLMs) address this limitation by enabling efficient parallel generation.
Through iterative denoising, DLMs can produce multiple tokens—or even full sequences—simultaneously, offering higher inference throughput and better alignment with modern parallel computing hardware <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Mathematical Reasoning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent efforts have significantly advanced LLMs’ performance on mathematical reasoning benchmarks. Two major directions have emerged: Chain-of-Thought prompting for enhancing multi-step reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kojima et al. (2022)</a></cite>, and code-based approaches that leverage program execution to address LLMs’ computational limitations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2023)</a></cite>. Building on these insights, we emphasize the complementary roles of explicit reasoning and executable code <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roziere et al. (2023)</a></cite> in solving math problems, while also acknowledging math-oriented pre-training as a means of improving general reasoning ability.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experiment</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Datasets and Implementation Details</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1 </span>Datasets</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our paradigm on both mathematical and coding tasks.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Math Dataset</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For mathematical reasoning, we consider two representative benchmarks: the competition-level <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AIME2025</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenCompass (2025)</a></cite> and the elementary-level <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GSM8K</span> dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al. (2021)</a></cite>. Specifically, we sample 20 problems from AIME2025 and 10 problems from GSM8K to assess model performance across varying levels of difficulty.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Code Dataset</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For code generation and reasoning, we construct our evaluation set by sampling 20 problems of <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hard</span> difficulty from the <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LeetCode</span> platform <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">LeetCode (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2 </span>Implementation Details</h4>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Overall setup.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt a two-stage inference pipeline that delegates <span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">think</span> to a non-autoregressive (NAR) diffusion language model and <span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">answer</span> to either the same NAR model or a strong autoregressive (AR) model. Concretely, we use Mercury <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Labs (2025)</a></cite> as the NAR reasoner to produce compact, explicit reasoning traces, and then instantiate two routing variants:
(i) <span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">NAR<math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>NAR</span> (<em id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mercury-follow</em>): Mercury consumes its own <span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">think</span> to produce the final <span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">answer</span>;
(ii) <span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">NAR<math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>AR</span> (<em id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">GPT5-follow</em>): GPT-5 (auto mode) consumes Mercury’s <span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">think</span> and emits the <span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">answer</span>.
Unless stated otherwise, we generate exactly one <span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">think</span> trace per instance (no self-consistency sampling) to isolate the effect of division-of-labor.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Experimental Results</h3>

<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Math</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Code</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Average</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">AIME2025</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">GSM8K</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">Leetcode</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" colspan="5"><span id="S3.T1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">NAR&amp;AR Framework (Our Paradigm)</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">GPT5+Mercury</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">50%</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">100%</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">95%</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">78%</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" colspan="5"><span id="S3.T1.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">AR&amp;AR Framework (Baseline Paradigm)</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Mercury+Mercury</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">10%</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">90%</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">75%</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">52%</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Main results on reasoning tasks like code and math. All numbers are success rates (%).
The <span id="S3.T1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span> entries denote the best results.
Our proposed NAR+AR paradigm (<span id="S3.T1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">think with Mercury, answer with GPT-5</span>) achieves consistent improvements over the AR+AR baseline.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate on three sets: AIME2025 (20 items), GSM8K (10 items), and LeetCode-Hard (20 items). The results are shown in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2 Experimental Results ‣ 3 Experiment ‣ Parallel Thinking, Sequential Answering: Bridging NAR and AR for Efficient Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
The pipeline first uses Mercury (NAR) to produce a compact think trace, then generates the final answer either with Mercury itself (NAR<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>NAR) or with GPT-5 in auto mode (NAR<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>AR). Scores are pass@1 success rates (%) and the overall number is the unweighted average across the three sets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, routing the final answer to GPT-5 given a Mercury-generated plan yields substantial improvements on both math and code tasks. The largest gains appear on competition-level math, while easier arithmetic-compositional problems approach a ceiling but still benefit from the NAR plan. Code tasks also see clear advantages, indicating that a compact, globally coherent plan helps the AR decoder realize precise outputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Across-the-board improvements: +40 on AIME2025 (50 vs. 10), +10 on GSM8K (100 vs. 90), +20 on LeetCode (95 vs. 75), with a +26 average lift (78 vs. 52).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Strong advantage in harder regimes (AIME2025), suggesting the NAR think stage mitigates long-horizon derivation errors before AR realization.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Near-ceiling GSM8K still benefits from the NAR plan, indicating complementary strengths even on simpler compositions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Code generation improves notably, supporting the division-of-labor design where NAR provides structured plans and AR ensures faithful surface realization.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
</section>
